ice brutality  racial injustice but one of the main  points on that list was a focus  on kind of grassroots programs being  able to bring in those that have been on  the ground fighting for these causes in  their cities  for quite some time and that was the  main thing they think that the mba  should have a dedicated coalition  people whose full-time job is to tackle  these  issues like voter suppression voting in  general  police reform education and that was  probably their main point to owners this  is something we can do that is tangible  change  and actionable item and that was the  main focus of the call today is creating  that coalition  whose job is to tackle these issues the  next question obviously i want to talk  about michael jordan he's obviously the  chairman  of the charlotte hornets he is also the  chairman of the labor union however  you said had he really stepped in on  your tweet and really  stepped in i don't want to say as a  mediator but just  shed some more light on mj's role  working between the owners and the  players bringing them closer together  yes i have been told by multiple people  that michael jordan has stepped up in  such a big way i said in the tweet for  him to be on the call  with these owners and really trying to  make sure that they see things from  different points of view  and him saying i am on this call not as  an  owner not as a former player but simply  as a black man  and that has been one of the themes that  has resonated throughout the bubble  here in orlando and you know it's not  just michael jordan i  have been told that doc rivers is  somebody that players have just  have always respected but have such a  newfound respect  for how vocal he has been throughout  this movement i asked him what  he has learned about coaching during  this time he said you know what taylor i  have always been political  but i haven't been political enough and  so what he's trying to do now  is make sure he is political enough for  a time that is calling for him to be  when you're talking about other former  players dwayne wade is somebody who has  been very active  in these calls talking to players he's  talking to them very  often and they are really trying to get  inside and outside voices on how they  can  advance this movement as much as  possible  all this being said with michael jordan  going to the older the older guys the  older owners in this nba  with him being the superstar that he is  do we think that we're going to get  these older owners to understand what  they need to be  they need to be done on their teams in  their in  in our cities to get this done  well you know that was one of my  questions right which is like okay  what's next  how optimistic are we that some of these  you know demands for lack of better word  on this list are met and everybody said  they were optimistic they felt that the  call was  productive which i think is the most  that you can ask for people listening to  you  people hearing you and people  understanding and there's this  overwhelming  feeling here that the owners are getting  it more than maybe people thought  before the bubble but it helps that  there are stars really backing it like i  said in those tweets lebron james was  the final voice on that call which was  really important because  that's what he stressed was that this  only works if we're all in this together  this only works if we care about this  genuinely at a human level when we're  not playing basketball  this only works if we believe in it and  players are starting to feel like owners  are seeing that more than they did  before  taylor obviously you spoke on sorry gp  just  just to add this the overall mood  obviously there's been a  a vast range of emotions over the last  24 to 48 hours  what's the overall mood from everybody  being from players owners  team personnel league execs down there  in orlando  honestly i think players are exhausted  and i think that it is a very layered  ex